  "state": {
    "case_id": "437",
    "case_summary": "A patient in their 70s presented with syncope that occurred 7 days before admission without preceding signs or symptoms, followed by exertional fatigue. Medical history includes hypertension, heart failure, and aortic valve replacement surgery in 2020 (complicated by prosthetic valve thrombosis). Current medications: warfarin, furosemide, metoprolol succinate, and spironolactone.\n\nHeart rate: 61 beats/minBlood pressure: 114/74 mm HgOxygen saturation: 96%Temperature: 36.6°CPhysical examination was unremarkable\n\n- ECG findings:  • Regular sinus rhythm, heart rate 75 beats/min  • Prolonged PR interval (240 milliseconds)  • Prolonged QRS duration (170 milliseconds)  • Morris index in lead V1  • Notched and monophasic R-wave in V1  • Absent S wave in leads I and aVL  • Left axis deviation (-81°)- Echocardiogram findings:  • Ejection fraction: 28%  • Akinesia of middle and basal segments of inferior and inferoseptal walls  • Diffuse left ventricular hypocontractility- 24-hour Holter monitoring:  • Regular rhythm  • Mean heart rate: 77 beats/min  • No pauses initially noted",
